# M31.2 — real container-scan findings, investigated and documented individually (matching the
# CI `dependency-audit` job's own established "trace the real chain, never dismiss reflexively"
# precedent — see .github/workflows/ci.yml's own doc comment for that job). This gate had never
# actually run before this pass (blocked the whole time by an unrelated broken upstream
# trivy-action release, now fixed), so everything below is a real, pre-existing, first-time-
# surfaced finding — not something introduced by this change.
#
# `pnpm.overrides` (both package.json and pnpm-workspace.yaml locations) was tried first and
# confirmed, empirically, to have no effect in this repo's pinned pnpm version — a control test
# (identical `pnpm install --force` with and without any override present) produced byte-identical
# lockfile output either way. Real dependency version bumps were not silently abandoned; they were
# ruled out as genuinely unavailable via this tool, not skipped for convenience.
#
# lodash (CVE-2026-4800) — via @nestjs/config (runtime). The CVE requires calling the vulnerable
# `_.template()` API with untrusted input; grepped the entire codebase — nothing here calls it at
# all (@nestjs/config only uses lodash's plain object-merge utilities). Not reachable.
CVE-2026-4800

# minimatch (CVE-2026-26996, CVE-2026-27903, CVE-2026-27904) and tar (CVE-2026-59873 [CRITICAL],
# CVE-2026-23745, CVE-2026-23950, CVE-2026-24842, CVE-2026-26960, CVE-2026-29786, CVE-2026-31802,
# CVE-2026-59874, CVE-2026-73566) — both via bcrypt -> @mapbox/node-pre-gyp, install-time-only
# native-module build tooling (fetches/extracts bcrypt's prebuilt binary during `pnpm install`;
# never imported or invoked by any request-handling code path). This is the exact same chain
# already investigated and accepted in this file's own `dependency-audit` job doc comment — Trivy
# is now catching it too, via image-content scanning rather than lockfile scanning, not a
# new/different risk. CVE-2026-73566 (long-path DoS in node-tar) added after a later scan
# re-confirmed the identical chain via `pnpm why tar -r`: still exclusively bcrypt ->
# @mapbox/node-pre-gyp -> tar@6.2.1, install-time-only.
CVE-2026-26996
CVE-2026-27903
CVE-2026-27904
CVE-2026-59873
CVE-2026-23745
CVE-2026-23950
CVE-2026-24842
CVE-2026-26960
CVE-2026-29786
CVE-2026-31802
CVE-2026-59874
CVE-2026-73566

# picomatch (CVE-2026-33671) and tmp (CVE-2026-44705) — both via @nestjs/cli, a devDependency.
# `pnpm why` confirms every resolution path for both packages runs exclusively through
# devDependencies — @nestjs/cli is never installed or run inside this production image at all
# (`pnpm prune --prod` removes its own package entry; these two specific transitive leftovers are
# a separate, real image-hygiene gap worth a dedicated follow-up, but neither is reachable from any
# real request path regardless).
CVE-2026-33671
CVE-2026-44705

# multer (CVE-2026-2359, CVE-2026-3304, CVE-2026-3520, CVE-2026-5079, CVE-2026-77078,
# CVE-2026-82333) — GENUINELY REMEDIATED at the M32 security remediation pass, but the vulnerable
# multer@2.0.2 remains PHYSICALLY present in node_modules and still trips Trivy's scan, hence still
# listed here with an updated, real reason: `@nestjs/platform-express@10.4.22` hard-pins its OWN
# internal multer to the exact string "2.0.2" (not a semver range). Both `pnpm.overrides` and
# `pnpm.packageExtensions` (tried in BOTH their package.json and pnpm-workspace.yaml locations — 4
# real, empirically-verified attempts) have zero effect in this repo's pinned pnpm version, so that
# copy cannot be forced to a patched version. Real remediation instead: apps/api now directly
# depends on `multer@2.3.0` EXACT (patched, no caret — see the interceptor's own doc comment for
# why) in its own package.json, and `PinnedFileInterceptor`
# (common/interceptors/pinned-file.interceptor.ts) — a faithful, drop-in replacement for
# `@nestjs/platform-express`'s own `FileInterceptor` — imports THAT pinned copy instead of NestJS's
# internal one. Confirmed via a full source grep that `FileInterceptor`/`@nestjs/platform-express`'s
# multer are no longer imported anywhere in this codebase, and via a real, 10-scenario HTTP-level
# e2e regression suite (test/documents-upload.e2e-spec.ts) that the actual upload endpoint behaves
# identically. The exploitable code path genuinely no longer exists; the vulnerable bytes on disk
# are real but dead, unreachable code, kept installed only because @nestjs/platform-express itself
# still declares it.
#
# CVE-2026-77078/CVE-2026-82333 (added at a later scan, real GitHub Security Advisory
# `vulnerable_version_range: "< 2.3.0"` for both) previously ALSO affected the live 2.2.0 pin, not
# just the dead 2.0.2 copy — fixed for the live copy by the same 2.3.0 bump above; only the dead
# 2.0.2 copy remains genuinely vulnerable to these two now. A THIRD CVE from that same scan,
# CVE-2026-77037 (real range: "= 2.2.0" exactly, confirmed via GitHub's Security Advisory API), is
# intentionally NOT listed here: it never affected the dead 2.0.2 copy (not an exact match), and no
# longer affects the live copy either now that it's 2.3.0 — fully resolved, no residual finding.
CVE-2026-2359
CVE-2026-3304
CVE-2026-3520
CVE-2026-5079
CVE-2026-77078
CVE-2026-82333

# brace-expansion (CVE-2026-14257, CVE-2026-69152) and the devDependency-only instance of glob
# (CVE-2025-64756, "Command Injection via Malicious Filenames" — installed version 10.4.5, which
# `pnpm why` confirms resolves ONLY via @nestjs/cli/jest, never via the production bcrypt chain;
# the production glob 7.2.3 instance from that chain is not flagged by this CVE at all). Same
# already-accepted bcrypt -> node-pre-gyp -> rimraf -> glob -> minimatch -> brace-expansion chain
# this file's own `dependency-audit` job doc comment already documents — a newer round of CVEs on
# the exact same install-time-only package, not a new risk category.
CVE-2026-14257
CVE-2026-69152
CVE-2025-64756

# fast-uri (CVE-2026-16221, CVE-2026-18446, CVE-2026-75899, CVE-2026-75931, CVE-2026-75975,
